<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<section xml:id="sec-6-7-memory-model">
  <title>Objects in Memory: The Stack and the Heap</title>

  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      It is well worth our time now to review the fundamentals of Java memory management, 
      discuss how other languages solve the same problems, and learn the causes of and 
      solutions to several common memory-related bugs. A robust understanding of how a Java 
      program operates behind the scenes will pay dividends: you will write correct code 
      from the start and avoid spending hours chasing memory-related crashes and semantic 
      errors.
    </p>
  </introduction>



  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-7-1-memory-areas">
    <title>Memory: Stack, Heap, and Static Area</title>

    
    <introduction>
      <p>
        In Section 1.4, we gave an overview of four areas of memory that a running program 
        maintains: the code segment, the static segment, the stack, and the heap. Your program's 
        data is stored in the last three: the static segment, the stack, and the heap. It is 
        critical to understand where a piece of data resides so that you know how to compare, 
        copy, and modify it correctly. If you try to compare objects using the equality 
        operators (<c>==</c> and <c>!=</c>), or if you accidentally declare an instance 
        variable <c>static</c>, your program will behave unpredictably.
      </p>
    </introduction>



    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-6-7-1-1-the-stack">
      <title>The Stack</title>

      <p>
        Each time a method is called, an activation record (or <em>frame</em>) is pushed 
        onto the call stack for that method. That frame contains all of the method's local 
        variables<mdash/>both parameters and variables declared within the method body.
      </p>

      <p>
        The value for each primitive variable is stored directly on the stack. The value 
        for each object variable is either <c>null</c> (a special literal indicating that 
        no memory address is held) or a memory address pointing to the object's data in the heap.
      </p>

      <p>
        When a method finishes executing, its frame is popped from the call stack, destroying 
        all of its local variables. If a method held a reference to an object, that object 
        still exists on the heap, even though the reference was removed from the stack. If 
        there are no other references to the object (for example, if the method created the 
        object and did not return it or store it elsewhere), then the object becomes 
        eligible for garbage collection.
      </p>

      <p>
        Consider the following method that we add to <c>PointDemo</c> and invoke with the 
        argument <c>p1</c>, after creating <c>p1</c> and <c>p2</c>:
      </p>

      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
    public static void memoryExample(Point parameterPoint) {
        Point localPoint;
        double x = parameterPoint.getX();
        double y = parameterPoint.getY();
        localPoint = new Point(x, y);
    }
      </program>

      <p>
        When this method is invoked, we can picture memory as shown in <xref ref="fig-memory-two-refs"/>: 
        on the call stack we have frames for <c>main</c> and <c>memoryExample</c>, each with 
        its own local variables. Both methods hold references pointing to the exact same 
        <c>Point</c> object in the heap (<c>p1</c> and <c>parameterPoint</c>).
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-memory-two-refs">
        <caption>Two references to the same object on the heap.</caption>
        <image source="figures/figure_6_8.png" width="95%">
          <description>
            A memory diagram showing Call Stack on the left and Heap on the right. On the stack, 
            the frame for main contains p1 holding 0x100 and p2 holding 0x200. Pushed above it, 
            the frame for memoryExample contains parameterPoint holding 0x100. Both p1 and 
            parameterPoint have arrows pointing to the same Point object at 0x100 on the heap.
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        After the last line of the method executes, we can picture memory as shown in 
        <xref ref="fig-memory-before-return"/>: there is now a third <c>Point</c> object in 
        the heap, referenced by <c>localPoint</c>, and the local primitive variables <c>x</c> 
        and <c>y</c> hold copied values.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-memory-before-return">
        <caption>Memory layout just before the method returns.</caption>
        <image source="figures/figure_6_9.png" width="95%">
          <description>
            A memory diagram showing memoryExample just before returning. Inside memoryExample, 
            x holds 3.0, y holds 4.0, and localPoint holds 0x300, pointing to a new Point object 
            at address 0x300 on the heap.
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Once <c>memoryExample</c> finishes executing, its frame is popped from the stack. 
        The local variables <c>x</c>, <c>y</c>, <c>localPoint</c>, and <c>parameterPoint</c> 
        are destroyed. Because <c>p1</c> in <c>main</c> still references the object at 
        <c>0x100</c>, that object remains active. However, because the only reference to the 
        third <c>Point</c> object was inside the popped stack frame, the object at address 
        <c>0x300</c> is now unreachable<mdash/>there are no remaining references pointing to 
        it from anywhere in memory. <xref ref="fig-memory-after-return"/> illustrates memory 
        after <c>memoryExample</c> returns.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-memory-after-return">
        <caption>Memory layout after method <c>memoryExample</c> returns.</caption>
        <image source="figures/figure_6_10.png" width="95%">
          <description>
            A memory diagram showing the call stack after memoryExample has popped. Only main 
            remains on the stack. In the heap, Point objects at 0x100 and 0x200 are active, while 
            the abandoned Point object at 0x300 is marked as unreachable with a red dashed border 
            and red cross for garbage collection.
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-6-7-1-2-the-heap">
      <title>The Heap</title>

      <p>
        Each time an object is created with the <c>new</c> keyword, space is allocated for 
        the object's data on the heap. (Remember that Java provides shortcut syntax that allows 
        <c>String</c> literals to be created without <c>new</c>, but they are still stored in 
        heap memory.) A reference to the object (its memory address) is placed in the variable 
        that the object is assigned to. If that variable is a local method variable, the 
        reference lives on the stack. An object can also be a field of another object. In that 
        case, the reference is stored inside an object on the heap.
      </p>

      <p>
        In <xref ref="fig-memory-ev-heap"/>, we see an <c>ElectricVehicle</c> object on the 
        heap. The value of each primitive <c>double</c> field is stored directly within the 
        object's memory block. Because the <c>model</c> field is a <c>String</c> (which is also 
        an object), that value resides in a separate location on the heap. The value stored 
        within the <c>ElectricVehicle</c> object is a reference<mdash/>the memory address of 
        that <c>String</c> object.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-memory-ev-heap">
        <caption>An <c>ElectricVehicle</c> object on the heap with composed <c>String</c> reference.</caption>
        <image source="figures/figure_6_11.png" width="95%">
          <description>
            A memory diagram of an ElectricVehicle object at address 0x400 on the heap. Primitive 
            fields batteryCapacityKwh, milesPerKwh, currentChargeKwh, and odometerMiles are stored 
            directly inside the object. The model field holds address 0x500 and points to an 
            independent String object containing 'Chevy Bolt EV' elsewhere on the heap.
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Objects stored in the heap remain there until they are removed by the automatic garbage 
        collector that runs periodically within the JVM. An object will only be collected if 
        there are zero active references pointing to it.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-6-7-1-3-static-segment">
      <title>The Static Segment</title>

      <p>
        Static fields<mdash/>fields that are part of a class blueprint and are shared by all 
        instances<mdash/>are stored in the static memory segment. These variables are allocated 
        when the JVM first encounters a line of code that references the class. Once created, 
        they exist for the entire duration of program execution.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-7-2-dereferencing">
    <title>Reference Variables and Implicit Dereferencing</title>

    
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Throughout this chapter, we have drawn arrows in our memory diagrams connecting reference 
        variables on the call stack to objects on the heap. But what is an arrow in physical 
        computer hardware?
      </p>

      <p>
        To understand how Java manipulates objects<mdash/>and to prepare you for the explicit 
        pointer syntax you will encounter in languages like C and C++<mdash/>we need to examine 
        what an address actually is and what happens when you use the dot operator (<c>.</c>).
      </p>
    </introduction>



    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-6-7-2-1-what-address-is">
      <title>What a Memory Address Really Is</title>

      <p>
        In physical RAM, every byte of memory has a unique numerical identifier called its 
        <em>memory address</em>. You can think of RAM as an immense row of post office boxes 
        or lockers, numbered sequentially from zero up to the total capacity of your computer's 
        memory.
      </p>

      <p>
        When you write:
      </p>

      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
Point p1 = new Point(3.0, 4.0);
      </program>

      <p>
        Two distinct operations occur in memory:
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            The <c>new</c> operator allocates a block of bytes on the heap to store the 
            <c>Point</c> object (its object header, its <c>x</c> field, and its <c>y</c> field), 
            beginning at a specific byte address (such as <c>0x004A1000</c>, which we simplify 
            as <c>0x100</c> in our diagrams).
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The variable <c>p1</c> is allocated on the call stack. The variable <c>p1</c> does 
            not contain the point's coordinates <c>3.0</c> and <c>4.0</c>. Instead, <c>p1</c> 
            stores that 64-bit integer address: <c>0x100</c>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        A reference variable is like a piece of paper with someone's street address written on 
        it. The piece of paper is not the house; it merely tells you where to find the house 
        if you travel to that location.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-6-7-2-2-meaning-dereferencing">
      <title>The Meaning of <q>Dereferencing</q></title>

      <p>
        In computer science, <em>dereferencing</em> means traveling to the memory address stored 
        inside a reference variable to access the actual data located there. It is the formal 
        term for <q>following the arrow</q> from the stack to the heap.
      </p>

      <p>
        In Java, dereferencing is completely <em>implicit</em> (automatic). Whenever you use 
        the dot operator on an object reference, Java performs two distinct operations in a 
        single step:
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <strong>It follows the address:</strong> It reads the memory address stored in 
            <c>p1</c> (<c>0x100</c>) and navigates to that location on the heap.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <strong>It selects the member:</strong> It accesses the requested field or invokes 
            the requested method (such as <c>getX()</c> or <c>translate()</c>) belonging to 
            that heap object.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        Because the dot operator handles both pointer navigation and member selection 
        simultaneously, Java programmers rarely have to think about raw memory addresses 
        during daily coding.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-6-7-2-3-cpp-pointers">
      <title>The C and C++ Connection: Explicit Pointers</title>

      <p>
        In lower-level languages like C and C++, memory is not hidden behind automatic language 
        features. C++ gives programmers direct, manual control over memory, requiring them to 
        choose explicitly how an object is stored:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <strong>As a direct value:</strong> <c>Point point;</c> allocates the point's data 
            directly on the stack without using a pointer.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <strong>As a pointer:</strong> <c>Point* p = new Point(3.0, 4.0);</c> creates a 
            pointer variable <c>p</c> that holds a heap memory address, just like in Java.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <strong>As a reference alias:</strong> <c>Point&amp; ref = point;</c> creates an 
            alternative name for an existing variable.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Because C++ makes this distinction explicit, if you hold a pointer variable <c>p</c>, 
        you cannot access its members using a simple dot operator. Writing <c>p.getX()</c> in 
        C++ produces a compiler error because <c>p</c> is an address, not an object.
      </p>

      <p>
        Instead, C and C++ require the programmer to dereference the pointer explicitly using 
        one of two notations:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <strong>The dereference operator (<c>*</c>):</strong> You must manually follow the 
            address using an asterisk, and then use the dot: <c>(*p).getX()</c>. The parentheses 
            are required because the dot operator has higher precedence than the asterisk.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <strong>The arrow operator (<c>-&gt;</c>):</strong> Because <c>(*p).</c> is cumbersome 
            to write, C and C++ provide the arrow operator as shorthand: <c>p-&gt;getX()</c>. 
            The arrow operator literally means: <q>follow this pointer address to the heap, and 
            then select this member.</q>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-6-7-2-4-java-pointers">
      <title>Java References Are Pointers with Automatic Dereferencing</title>

      <p>
        When you transition to C++, pointer syntax can feel intimidating at first. However, 
        you already understand how it works because every object variable in Java is secretly 
        a pointer under the hood.
      </p>

      <p>
        Java's designers simply decided to eliminate the pointer syntax (<c>*</c> and <c>-&gt;</c>) 
        to make the language safer and easier to learn. In Java, writing:
      </p>

      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
p1.getX();
      </program>

      <p>
        performs the exact same mechanical operation as writing:
      </p>

      <program language="c++">
p1-&gt;getX();
      </program>

      <p>
        in C++. Recognizing that Java's dot operator implicitly follows an address to the heap 
        will make learning a language with pointers feel familiar. More importantly, it unlocks 
        the answers to critical memory questions: why assigning one object variable to another 
        does not copy the object, and why calling a method on <c>null</c> crashes your program.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-7-3-null-pointer">
    <title>The <c>null</c> Reference and <c>NullPointerException</c></title>

    <p>
      <xref ref="listing-rectangle-class"/> is a <c>Rectangle</c> class that contains a 
      <c>Point</c> object as a field. Take a moment to read through the class, and then we will 
      examine what <c>null</c> means and how it relates to one of the most common bugs in 
      computing: the <c>NullPointerException</c>.
    </p>

    <listing xml:id="listing-rectangle-class">
      <caption>The <c>Rectangle</c> class</caption>
      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
/**
 * Represents a two-dimensional rectangle defined by an anchor Point 
 * at its top-left corner, a width, and a height.
 */
public class Rectangle {
 
    private Point topLeft;
    private double width;
    private double height;

    /**
     * Constructs a new Rectangle with a specified top-left anchor point 
     * and dimensions.
     *
     * Precondition: width > 0.0 &amp;&amp; height > 0.0
     * Postcondition: The rectangle is initialized with the given dimensions,
     *                and topLeft is initialized at coordinates (x, y).
     *
     * @param x the x coordinate of the rectangle left
     * @param y the y coordinate of the rectangle top
     * @param width the horizontal width of the rectangle
     * @param height the vertical height of the rectangle
     */
    public Rectangle(double x, double y, double width, double height) {
        assert width > 0.0 &amp;&amp; height > 0.0 : "Dimensions must be positive";
        this.topLeft = new Point(x, y);
        this.width = width;
        this.height = height;
    }

    /**
     * Calculates the distance from the coordinate origin (0.0, 0.0) 
     * to this rectangle's top-left corner by delegating to the Point object.
     *
     * Precondition: topLeft != null
     * Postcondition: Returns the Euclidean distance from (0, 0) to top-left.
     *
     * @return the distance from (0.0, 0.0) to the top-left corner
     */
    public double distanceFromOrigin() {
        assert this.topLeft != null : "Cannot calc distance: top-left point is null";
        return this.topLeft.distanceFromOrigin();
    }

    /**
     * Moves the rectangle to a new top-left anchor position.
     *
     * Precondition: None
     * Postcondition: The rectangle's top-left corner is a new Point at (x, y);
     *                width and height are unchanged.
     *
     * @param x the x coordinate of the rectangle left
     * @param y the y coordinate of the rectangle top
     */
    public void moveTo(double x, double y) {
        this.topLeft = new Point(x, y);
    }

    /**
     * Returns the current top-left anchor Point.
     *
     * @return the top-left corner Point reference
     */
    public Point getTopLeft() {
        return this.topLeft;
    }
}
      </program>
    </listing>

    <p>
      A <c>Rectangle</c> object is composed of a <c>width</c> and <c>height</c> (<c>double</c>), 
      and a <c>Point</c> object that establishes its position. There is one constructor that 
      initializes the width and height, and initializes <c>topLeft</c> by instantiating a new 
      <c>Point</c> from the <c>x</c> and <c>y</c> arguments. There is one mutator<mdash/><c>moveTo</c><mdash/>that 
      creates a new <c>Point</c> at new coordinates and assigns it to <c>topLeft</c>. There are 
      two accessors: <c>getTopLeft</c>, and <c>distanceFromOrigin</c>, which delegates the distance 
      calculation directly to the composed <c>Point</c>:
    </p>

    <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
return this.topLeft.distanceFromOrigin();
    </program>

    <p>
      <xref ref="listing-rectangle-demo"/> is a driver program called <c>RectangleDemo</c> 
      that creates a <c>Rectangle</c> and moves it.
    </p>

    <listing xml:id="listing-rectangle-demo">
      <caption>The <c>RectangleDemo</c> class</caption>
      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
public class RectangleDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Creates a Rectangle and prints its distance from the origin.
        Rectangle rect1 = new Rectangle(3.0, 4.0, 10.0, 20.0);
        System.out.println(rect1.distanceFromOrigin());
        
        // Moves it and prints its distance from the origin.
        rect1.moveTo(5.0, 12.0);
        System.out.println(rect1.distanceFromOrigin());
    }
}
      </program>
    </listing>

    <p>
      This program displays the rectangle's distance from the origin after construction and 
      after moving, producing the following output:
    </p>

    <console>
      <output>
5.0
13.0
      </output>
    </console>

    <p>
      <xref ref="fig-rect-demo-line4"/> visualizes the stack and heap after line 4 executes. 
      You can see a reference in <c>main</c> on the stack pointing to the <c>Rectangle</c> 
      object <c>rect1</c> on the heap. The <c>Rectangle</c> object on the heap references a 
      <c>Point</c> object for its <c>topLeft</c> field.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-rect-demo-line4">
      <caption>The stack and heap after line 4 in <c>RectangleDemo.main</c> executes.</caption>
      <image source="figures/figure_6_12.png" width="95%">
        <description>
          A memory diagram showing Call Stack on the left and Heap on the right. In main, rect1 
          at 0x7FE8 holds address 0x300. On the heap, the Rectangle object at address 0x300 contains 
          width 10.0, height 20.0, and a topLeft field holding address 0x100. An arrow from topLeft 
          arcs out to a Point object at address 0x100 containing x equal to 3.0 and y equal to 4.0.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      After line 8 executes, the <c>Rectangle</c> object's <c>topLeft</c> field references a 
      new <c>Point</c> object at address <c>0x200</c>. Because the original <c>Point</c> at 
      <c>0x100</c> no longer has any references pointing to it, it is marked for garbage 
      collection, as illustrated in <xref ref="fig-rect-demo-line8"/>.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-rect-demo-line8">
      <caption>Memory layout after line 8 executes (<c>moveTo</c> and garbage collection).</caption>
      <image source="figures/figure_6_13.png" width="95%">
        <description>
          A memory diagram showing memory after rect1.moveTo(5.0, 12.0) executes. The Rectangle 
          at address 0x300 now holds 0x200 in its topLeft field, pointing to a new Point object at 
          0x200 with x 5.0 and y 12.0. The original Point object at 0x100 has zero incoming references 
          and is marked as unreachable for garbage collection with a dashed border and red cross.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      As we discussed in Section 6.6.1, when an object is created, its fields are initialized 
      to default values. There is a special keyword for an unassigned object reference: 
      <c>null</c>. It means the reference variable does not point to any object at all. In 
      our <c>Rectangle</c> class, it makes no sense for the <c>Point</c> field to be <c>null</c>, 
      so we initialize it in the constructor and ensure it is never set to <c>null</c>.
    </p>

    <p>
      For some classes, an object reference may legitimately be <c>null</c> if it represents 
      an optional component. For example, a <c>Name</c> class might have an optional 
      <c>middleName</c> field. In methods that use that field, the programmer must verify that 
      the reference is not <c>null</c> before dereferencing it:
    </p>

    <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
// Safe defensive check before dereferencing:
if (this.middleName != null) {
    System.out.println("Middle initial: " + this.middleName.charAt(0));
}
    </program>

    <p>
      A <c>null</c> reference cannot be dereferenced<mdash/>the JVM cannot follow a <c>null</c> 
      address to an object on the heap because no object exists there. If we attempt to use the 
      dot operator on a <c>null</c> reference, the JVM throws a <c>NullPointerException</c>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Suppose we make the common mistake of forgetting to initialize <c>topLeft</c> in our 
      constructor:
    </p>

    <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
    // Buggy constructor: forgot to initialize topLeft!
    public Rectangle(double width, double height) {
        this.width = width;
        this.height = height;
    }
    </program>

    <p>
      The <c>topLeft</c> field would retain the zeroed-out <c>null</c> value assigned to it 
      when memory was allocated on the heap. <xref ref="fig-rect-null-pointer"/> depicts memory 
      after this modified constructor executes.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-rect-null-pointer">
      <caption>Memory layout after the modified constructor leaves <c>topLeft</c> as <c>null</c>.</caption>
      <image source="figures/figure_6_14.png" width="95%">
        <description>
          A memory diagram showing a Rectangle object at address 0x300 on the heap whose topLeft 
          field explicitly contains null with a slash-through circle symbol. A red callout box 
          notes that attempting to invoke methods on this null reference will crash with a 
          NullPointerException.
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      If we run with assertions disabled, or if we omit our defensive assertion, attempting to 
      invoke <c>rect1.distanceFromOrigin()</c> causes Java to throw an immediate exception:
    </p>

    <console>
      <output>
Exception in thread "main" java.lang.NullPointerException: Cannot invoke "Point.distanceFromOrigin()" because "this.topLeft" is null
    at Rectangle.distanceFromOrigin(Rectangle.java:42)
    at RectangleDemo.main(RectangleDemo.java:5)
      </output>
    </console>

    <p>
      Line 42 of <c>Rectangle.java</c> attempts to invoke <c>distanceFromOrigin</c> on 
      <c>topLeft</c>, but <c>topLeft</c> contains <c>null</c>. Because <c>null</c> is not an 
      object, the JVM halts with a <c>NullPointerException</c>.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-7-4-aliasing">
    <title>Object Aliasing: Assignment (<c>=</c>) and Method Arguments</title>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-6-7-4-1-pass-by-value-objects">
      <title>What Pass-by-Value Means with Objects</title>

      <p>
        In Section 6.7.1.1, we saw a method that accepted a <c>Point</c> object as a parameter:
      </p>

      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
    public static void memoryExample(Point parameterPoint) {
        ...
    }
      </program>

      <p>
        When we invoke a method, Java evaluates the arguments and copies their values directly 
        into the matching parameter variables on the call stack. For primitive types, this copies 
        the raw data (such as an <c>int</c> or a <c>double</c>). For reference types, Java follows 
        the exact same rule: the value stored in the argument variable is copied directly into 
        the parameter variable on the stack. However, because the value stored in an object 
        variable is a memory address, what gets copied is that reference<mdash/>not the object's 
        data. The object itself remains on the heap.
      </p>

      <p>
        The implications are profound: while reassigning a primitive parameter variable has no 
        effect on the caller, invoking a mutator method through a reference parameter directly 
        modifies the object on the heap.
      </p>

      <p>
        Suppose we add the following <c>reflectPoint</c> method to <c>PointDemo.java</c>:
      </p>

      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
    /**
     * Reflects the point across the y axis.
     * Precondition: p != null
     * Postcondition: p.getX() == -old(p.getX()) &amp;&amp; p.getY() == old(p.getY())
     * 
     * @param p The Point to reflect.
     */
    public static void reflectPoint(Point p) {
        assert p != null : "A nonexistent point cannot be reflected.";
        p.translate(-2 * p.getX(), 0);
    }
      </program>

      <p>
        And suppose we invoke <c>reflectPoint(p1)</c> in <c>main</c>:
      </p>

      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
Point p1 = new Point(3.0, 4.0);
Point p2 = new Point(5.0, 12.0);
reflectPoint(p1);
      </program>

      <p>
        The <c>reflectPoint</c> method receives a copy of the address stored in <c>p1</c>. Both 
        <c>p1</c> in <c>main</c>'s frame and <c>p</c> in <c>reflectPoint</c>'s frame reference 
        the exact same heap memory block. When <c>reflectPoint</c> calls <c>p.translate(-6.0, 0)</c>, 
        Java follows the pointer to the heap and updates <c>x</c> from <c>3.0</c> to <c>-3.0</c> 
        directly in heap memory. <xref ref="fig-memory-reflect-point"/> illustrates memory after 
        the <c>translate</c> method is invoked inside <c>reflectPoint</c>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-memory-reflect-point">
        <caption>Memory layout after <c>reflectPoint</c> mutates the object referenced by <c>p1</c>.</caption>
        <image source="figures/figure_6_15.png" width="95%">
          <description>
            A memory diagram showing main on the stack with p1 holding 0x100, and reflectPoint on 
            the stack with parameter p holding 0x100. Both variables point to the same Point object 
            at address 0x100 on the heap, whose x field has been mutated from 3.0 to -3.0.
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        This may initially feel like a violation of pass-by-value. Pass-by-value guarantees that 
        the caller's reference variable cannot be redirected to point to a different object.
      </p>

      <p>
        To see this, suppose we wrote a flawed version, <c>badReflectPoint</c>, attempting to 
        create a new reflected object rather than mutating the existing one:
      </p>

      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
public static void badReflectPoint(Point p) {
    // Reassigns the LOCAL parameter p to a new heap object;
    // does NOT change p1 in main!
    assert p != null : "A nonexistent point cannot be reflected.";
    p = new Point(-p.getX(), p.getY());
}
      </program>

      <p>
        When <c>badReflectPoint(p1)</c> executes, the parameter variable <c>p</c> initially 
        receives a reference to the object at <c>0x100</c>. But the method then allocates a new 
        <c>Point</c> object and assigns that new memory address to <c>p</c>. This assignment 
        updates only the local parameter in <c>badReflectPoint</c>'s stack frame. It has zero 
        impact on <c>p1</c> in <c>main</c>, which still references the original object at 
        <c>0x100</c>. <xref ref="fig-memory-bad-reflect"/> illustrates memory after <c>p</c> 
        is reassigned.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-memory-bad-reflect">
        <caption>Memory layout after <c>badReflectPoint</c> reassigns its local parameter.</caption>
        <image source="figures/figure_6_16.png" width="95%">
          <description>
            A memory diagram showing badReflectPoint where parameter p has been reassigned to a 
            new Point object at address 0x300. The original Point object at 0x100 remains unchanged 
            at coordinates (3.0, 4.0), still referenced by p1 in main.
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        When <c>badReflectPoint</c> returns, its stack frame is popped, destroying local variable 
        <c>p</c>. Because no other reference points to the newly allocated object at <c>0x300</c>, 
        that object is immediately abandoned for garbage collection, while <c>p1</c> in <c>main</c> 
        remains at <m>(3.0, 4.0)</m>, completely unaffected.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-6-7-4-2-aliasing-assignment">
      <title>Object Aliasing Through the Assignment Operator</title>

      <p>
        The exact same phenomenon occurs within a single stack frame whenever we use the 
        assignment operator (<c>=</c>) with reference variables.
      </p>

      <p>
        When working with primitive types, copying duplicates the raw value:
      </p>

      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
int score1 = 100;
int score2 = score1; // Copies the integer 100 into score2

score2 = 85;         // Overwrites score2 with 85
System.out.println(score1); // Still prints 100
      </program>

      <p>
        When working with reference variables, Java follows the exact same mechanical rule: it 
        copies the value from the right-hand side into the container on the left-hand side. But 
        the value stored in a reference variable is a memory address:
      </p>

      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
Point p1 = new Point(3.0, 4.0);
Point p3 = p1; // Copies the address stored in p1 into p3
      </program>

      <p>
        Line 2 does not invoke <c>new</c>, and it does not manufacture a second <c>Point</c> 
        object. There is still only one <c>Point</c> object in the heap. We now have two distinct 
        variables on the call stack<mdash/><c>p1</c> and <c>p3</c><mdash/>that both hold the 
        address <c>0x100</c>. When two or more reference variables store the same memory address, 
        they are called <em>aliases</em>. <xref ref="fig-memory-aliasing-assignment"/> shows 
        memory after this assignment executes.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-memory-aliasing-assignment">
        <caption>Memory layout after an assignment (<c>=</c>) creates an alias.</caption>
        <image source="figures/figure_6_17.png" width="95%">
          <description>
            A memory diagram showing main on the stack with two variables, p1 and p3, both holding 
            address 0x100. Both variables have arrows pointing to the same single Point object at 
            0x100 on the heap.
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Because both variables point to the same physical memory on the heap, any mutator method 
        called through either alias modifies the shared object:
      </p>

      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
p3.translate(1.0, 2.0); // Shifts the object at 0x100 by (1.0, 2.0)

System.out.println(p3.getX()); // Prints 4.0
System.out.println(p1.getX()); // Prints 4.0!
      </program>

      <p>
        Understanding that reference variables hold memory addresses also clears up another 
        common point of confusion: the equality operator (<c>==</c>). When applied to reference 
        variables, <c>==</c> compares the memory addresses stored in the variables, not the 
        data stored inside the heap objects:
      </p>

      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
Point p1 = new Point(3.0, 4.0);
Point p2 = new Point(3.0, 4.0);
Point p3 = p1;

System.out.println(p1 == p3); // true  (both hold address 0x100; they are aliases)
System.out.println(p1 == p2); // false (0x100 != 0x200; they are distinct objects!)
      </program>

      <p>
        Even though <c>p1</c> and <c>p2</c> have identical coordinate fields <m>(3.0, 4.0)</m>, 
        they reside in separate heap allocations. Because <c>0x100 != 0x200</c>, <c>p1 == p2</c> 
        evaluates to <c>false</c>.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-7-5-copy-constructors">
    <title>True Object Duplication: The Copy Constructor</title>

    
    <introduction>
      <p>
        If the assignment operator cannot duplicate an object, how do we create an independent 
        copy of data in the heap?
      </p>

      <p>
        Duplicating an object requires resolving critical design questions: should composed objects 
        be shared (a <em>shallow copy</em>), or should new independent objects be allocated (a 
        <em>deep copy</em>)? Because these decisions depend on the class design and invariants, 
        Java places control in the hands of the class author through a specialized pattern: 
        the <em>copy constructor</em>.
      </p>
    </introduction>



    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-6-7-5-1-copy-fundamentals">
      <title>Copy Constructor Fundamentals</title>

      <p>
        A copy constructor is a constructor that takes a single parameter of its own class 
        type and constructs an independent duplicate on the heap.
      </p>

      <p>
        Let's add a copy constructor to the <c>Point</c> class:
      </p>

      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
    /**
     * Constructs a new Point with the same coordinates as an existing Point.
     *
     * Precondition: toCopy != null
     *
     * @param toCopy the Point object to copy
     */
    public Point(Point toCopy) {
        assert toCopy != null : "Cannot copy a null Point";
        this(toCopy.x, toCopy.y);
    }
      </program>

      <p>
        Here we follow the constructor chaining pattern, invoking the primary two-parameter 
        constructor from within the copy constructor. Within a class definition, Java allows 
        direct access to the private fields of another object of the same class (such as 
        <c>toCopy.x</c> and <c>toCopy.y</c>).
      </p>

      <p>
        Code in <c>main</c> that invokes the copy constructor:
      </p>

      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
Point p1 = new Point(3.0, 4.0);
Point p2 = new Point(5.0, 12.0);
Point p3 = new Point(p1);
      </program>

      <p>
        <xref ref="fig-memory-copy-constructor"/> illustrates memory after this code executes. 
        <c>p1</c> and <c>p3</c> have identical coordinates, but reside in distinct heap allocations.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-memory-copy-constructor">
        <caption>Memory layout after invoking a copy constructor.</caption>
        <image source="figures/figure_6_18.png" width="95%">
          <description>
            A memory diagram showing three distinct Point objects on the heap. Variable p1 holds 
            0x100 (x=3.0, y=4.0), p2 holds 0x200 (x=5.0, y=12.0), and p3 holds 0x300 containing 
            independent fields x=3.0 and y=4.0 copied from p1.
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-6-7-5-2-deep-vs-shallow">
      <title>Shallow vs. Deep Copying</title>

      <p>
        A <c>Rectangle</c> object is composed of primitive fields (<c>width</c> and <c>height</c>) 
        and an object reference (<c>Point topLeft</c>). If we write a copy constructor that 
        simply copies field values directly, we make a <em>shallow copy</em>:
      </p>

      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
    // Shallow copy constructor: copies references!
    public Rectangle(Rectangle toCopy) {
        assert toCopy != null : "Cannot copy a null Rectangle.";
        this.topLeft = toCopy.topLeft;
        this.width = toCopy.width;
        this.height = toCopy.height;
    }
      </program>

      <p>
        In a shallow copy, the new object has its own primitive fields, but reference fields 
        become aliases to the composed objects of the original instance, as shown in 
        <xref ref="fig-shallow-copy-rect"/>. If one rectangle moves its <c>topLeft</c> point, 
        the other rectangle is unintentionally moved as well!
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-shallow-copy-rect">
        <caption>A shallow copy of <c>Rectangle</c> sharing a single <c>Point</c> object.</caption>
        <image source="figures/figure_6_19.png" width="95%">
          <description>
            A memory diagram illustrating a shallow copy. On the stack, rect1 references 0x200 
            and rect2 references 0x300. In the heap, both distinct Rectangle objects have independent 
            width and height fields, but both hold address 0x100 in their topLeft fields, arcing to 
            the exact same shared Point object at address 0x100.
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Making a <em>deep copy</em> eliminates this shared-reference side effect. To make a 
        deep copy, we allocate a brand-new <c>Point</c> object with the same coordinates, ensuring 
        both primitive and reference fields are completely independent:
      </p>

      <program language="java" line-numbers="yes">
    /**
     * Constructs a new Rectangle that is a deep copy of the parameter.
     *
     * Precondition: toCopy != null &amp;&amp; toCopy.topLeft != null
     * Postcondition: this.width == toCopy.width &amp;&amp;
     *                this.height == toCopy.height &amp;&amp;
     *                this.topLeft.getX() == toCopy.topLeft.getX() &amp;&amp;
     *                this.topLeft.getY() == toCopy.topLeft.getY() &amp;&amp;
     *                this.topLeft != toCopy.topLeft
     *
     * @param toCopy The other Rectangle to copy.
     */
    public Rectangle(Rectangle toCopy) {
        assert toCopy != null &amp;&amp; toCopy.topLeft != null : 
            "Cannot copy a null Rectangle or a Rectangle with a null Point.";
        this(toCopy.topLeft.getX(), toCopy.topLeft.getY(), toCopy.width, toCopy.height);
    }
      </program>

      <p>
        <xref ref="fig-deep-copy-rect"/> illustrates memory when using a deep copy constructor. 
        Both rectangles are now completely isolated.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-deep-copy-rect">
        <caption>A deep copy of <c>Rectangle</c> with completely independent composed objects.</caption>
        <image source="figures/figure_6_20.png" width="95%">
          <description>
            A memory diagram illustrating a deep copy. On the stack, rect1 references 0x200 and 
            rect2 references 0x300. In the heap, rect1 at 0x200 references original Point 0x100, 
            while rect2 at 0x300 references an independent cloned Point object at address 0x400. 
            Modifying one point has zero impact on the other.
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-6-7-6-garbage-collection">
    <title>Object Lifetime and Automatic Garbage Collection</title>

    
    <introduction>
      <p>
        When a local variable goes out of scope on the stack, its lifetime ends immediately and 
        its memory is reclaimed by the CPU. But heap memory does not have a scope tied to curly 
        braces (<c>{ }</c>). An object on the heap can outlive the method that instantiated it<mdash/>as 
        long as some variable somewhere in the program still holds its address.
      </p>
    </introduction>



    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-6-7-6-1-reachability">
      <title>The Definition of <q>Unreachable</q></title>

      <p>
        Java determines the end of an object's lifetime through <em>reachability</em>. An object 
        is considered reachable if your running program can still access it by following active 
        reference variables beginning from:
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Local variables currently sitting on the call stack in any active method frame.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Static reference fields belonging to loaded classes in the static segment.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        An object becomes <em>unreachable</em> the moment there is no longer any valid chain of 
        references leading to it. Once unreachable, a program can never access or inspect it again.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-6-7-6-2-garbage-collector">
      <title>The Garbage Collector: The JVM's Automated Cleanup Crew</title>

      <p>
        In low-level languages like C and C++, allocating memory requires the programmer to 
        manually release it when finished:
      </p>

      <program language="c++">
// In C++: Manual memory management
Point* ptr = new Point(3.0, 4.0); // Programmer manually allocates
delete ptr;                       // Programmer must remember to manually free!
      </program>

      <p>
        Manual memory management places a heavy cognitive burden on the developer, giving rise 
        to two notorious categories of software bugs:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <strong>Memory Leaks:</strong> If a programmer forgets to call <c>delete</c>, the 
            abandoned object remains in RAM indefinitely. Over time, the application consumes 
            all available memory until the operating system forcibly terminates it.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <strong>Dangling Pointers and Segmentation Faults:</strong> If a programmer calls 
            <c>delete</c> too early while another part of the program is still using that memory 
            address, the remaining reference becomes a <em>dangling pointer</em>. Attempting to 
            use a dangling pointer causes data corruption, security exploits, or crashes.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Java was deliberately designed to eliminate these errors by employing an automated 
        runtime background service known as the <em>Garbage Collector (GC)</em>.
      </p>

      <p>
        The JVM continuously monitors the heap behind the scenes. When the garbage collector 
        detects that an object has zero active references pointing to it, it marks the object 
        as unreachable (as illustrated with the red cross in <xref ref="fig-memory-after-return"/>) 
        and reclaims that memory block so it can be reused for future <c>new</c> expressions.
      </p>
    </subsubsection>
  </subsection>
</section>